    docs(master): 15, 16, 22 and 26 verified; 18 stays RED because its adapter cannot pass; nine rows filed
    
    Third writer wave. Four VERIFIED, one deliberately RED:
    
      15 claude-code installer  VERIFIED 294 locators
      16 codex installer        VERIFIED 409 locators
      22 lib.sh                 VERIFIED — the helper contract measured in bytes
      26 node-create.sh         VERIFIED 359 locators, staged set measured item by item
      18 opencode installer     RED — a check-only adapter whose non_interactive row provably cannot pass
      40 modules, 12 VERIFIED, 0 errors, MASTER-DOC: PASS
    
    18 is the interesting one: CF-240 is LIVE (line 25 still does `--help 2>/dev/null | grep -qi 'run|exec|serve'` while opencode writes help to stderr — measured 0 B stdout / 4 285 B stderr), it installs no generation at all, and the only v2 gate in the tree is the battery's. Also: tests/harness/vars-test.sh does not exist on origin/main — it lives only on unmerged wt/cf-vars.
    
    What documenting these found, filed as CF-277..CF-285:
      * install-codex.sh builds `rm -rf` from an unsanitised CF_RUN_ID; a patched copy deleted $HOME/victim.
      * its isolated-credential branch cannot execute at all (`$VAR[CF_RUN_ID]` is an unbound variable), so
        the one path that keeps a key out of the host's ~/.codex has probably never run.
      * lib.sh fetch_verified RETURNS 0 AFTER A FAILED INSTALL — mkdir denied and mv failed are flattened by
        `chmod || true`, and install-go/install-codex branch on that status. The helper the ladder trusts to
        prove a digest can report a verified artifact that is not there.
      * lib.sh have() ends in `|| true`, so every `b=$(have x) || { bad ... }` guard is dead code; bad()
        returns 0, so `bad ... || exit 1` never exits; FAILED is lost in a subshell, and the --apply
        preflight runs in one at :617.
      * `node-create.sh --harness codex` cannot be provisioned at all: its validator demands
        harness/harness-vars.sh, which exists nowhere while the adapter guards it with [ -f ].
    
    Corrections to our own record, both from measurement: CF-267 said a missing lib.sh makes the provisioner
    exit 0 — it exits 1 (fail-closed on status, fail-open on evidence), so the row is retitled and its false
    premise removed. And my brief for module 15 asserted the installer checks maintainers, uses --prefix on
    every npm call, and never invokes npm on a second run: none of the three is true of the source. A brief
    is a hypothesis; the seat's job is to refute it, and this one did.
